# Rule: SwiftData Concurrency Model

Sources

https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/concurrencysupport
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/modelactor()
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/modelactor
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/defaultserialmodelexecutor
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/serialmodelexecutor
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/modelexecutor
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/modelcontext

Scope

Applies to:

- `@ModelActor` usage and executor isolation
- cross-thread/cross-actor model access patterns
- identifier-based re-resolution workflows
- main-actor vs background-actor persistence boundaries

Intent

Prevent thread-safety violations by enforcing context isolation and actor-based
coordination for all nontrivial SwiftData operations.

Core Principle

Cross-thread SwiftData work must run through isolated contexts and actor-coordinated executors.

Implementation Rules

- Use `@ModelActor` for serial, mutually exclusive background data operations.
- Keep context-bound model instances within their owning context/actor boundary.
- Pass stable identifiers/value snapshots across boundaries; refetch models in the receiving context.
- Use `DefaultSerialModelExecutor` semantics through model actors for background safety.
- Do not force sendability workarounds on model types to bypass boundary issues.
- Keep UI-bound context work on main actor and heavy persistence tasks off main actor.

Canonical Patterns

```swift
@ModelActor
actor BillingStoreActor {
    func load(id: PersistentIdentifier) -> Invoice? {
        self[id, as: Invoice.self]
    }
}
```

Anti-Patterns

- Passing live model instances between unrelated contexts/actors.
- Doing heavy batch persistence work on main-actor UI paths.
- Relying on unchecked sendability to suppress concurrency violations.
- Creating ad hoc background contexts without clear executor isolation.

Checklist

- Are background writes routed through model actors?
- Are cross-actor payloads value-based (IDs/snapshots) rather than model references?
- Are model instances used only in owning context?
- Are executor/isolation boundaries explicit in code review?

Architectural Implications

- Concurrency boundaries are data integrity boundaries; violations create nondeterministic crashes and corruption risk.
- Model actor design should mirror service ownership and background workflow needs.
- Cross-actor data contracts should be value-based and explicit.

Advanced Notes

- Model instances are context-bound; resolve by persistent identifier inside receiving actor/context.
- `DefaultSerialModelExecutor` patterns provide safe serialized access for background tasks.
- Main-context UI and background persistence paths must remain separated by design.

Integration With Architecture

- Place model actors near persistence service boundaries in `Packages/Data`.
- Keep DI/bootstrap code responsible for creating actor-backed services in non-main contexts.
- Review all background import/sync workflows for value-snapshot boundary compliance.

Enforcement Summary

Always prefer:

- `@ModelActor` for background persistence serialization
- value snapshot boundary crossing
- in-context model resolution by identifier
- explicit executor ownership in architecture

Never allow:

- passing live models across actor/context boundaries
- main-thread heavy persistence operations
- unchecked sendability workarounds on model types
- implicit concurrent writes without serialized model actor path
